Significance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Khālid ibn ʿAbd al‐Malik al‐Marwarrūdhī played a pivotal role in the Abbasid Caliphate's scientific advancements, particularly in the direct measurement of the Earth's circumference, yielding results remarkably close to modern values. His work significantly contributed to the empirical foundation of astronomy and geodesy, impacting subsequent cartography and scientific inquiry for centuries.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-11-10T17:23:09Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#42861733994", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Khālid ibn ʿAbd al‐Malik al‐Marwarrūdhī was a 9th-century Baghdadi astronomer.", "newValue": "Khālid ibn ʿAbd al‐Malik al‐Marwarrūdhī was a preeminent astronomer and geographer of the early 9th century CE, instrumental in the scientific blossoming of the Abbasid Caliphate under Caliph al-Ma'mun. His contributions were central to the House of Wisdom's ambitious projects to refine astronomical observations and establish the Earth's dimensions, marking a significant advancement in Islamic science and globally. He is particularly renowned for his role in the first direct measurement of the Earth's circumference by Islamic scholars, a feat that combined theoretical knowledge with practical, large-scale surveying.

Working within the vibrant intellectual environment of Baghdad, Khālid ibn ʿAbd al‐Malik participated in two groundbreaking expeditions commissioned by Caliph al-Ma'mun around 827 CE. One team traveled north from Tadmur (Palmyra) toward Raqqa, while another measured south from Kufa, aiming to determine the length of a degree of latitude. By meticulously measuring the distance between two points where the elevation of the celestial pole differed by one degree, they derived a value for the Earth's circumference that was remarkably close to modern estimates, ranging from 24,000 to 25,000 miles. Beyond geodesy, al-Marwarrūdhī also contributed to the development of astronomical instruments and the compilation of *zij* tables, which provided essential data for celestial calculations.

The meticulous work of al-Marwarrūdhī and his contemporaries profoundly influenced subsequent Islamic and European scientific thought. Their empirical approach to geodesy and astronomy laid foundational groundwork for later cartography and navigation, demonstrating the practical application of Greek mathematical traditions combined with innovative observational techniques. The precision achieved in measuring the Earth's circumference stood as a testament to the scientific rigor of the Abbasid era, inspiring generations of scholars and explorers."}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-10T17:23:09Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#42861733994", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "900 CE"}, {"title": "Accumulation of mathematical knowledge and formal systems", "type": "Idea", "year": "900 CE"}], "newValue": ["The Abbasid Caliphate's financial and intellectual patronage under Caliph al-Ma'mun created an environment ripe for scientific inquiry.", "The translation movement of Greek scientific texts provided a rich foundation of astronomical and mathematical knowledge for scholars like al-Marwarrūdhī to build upon.", "The practical needs of Islamic society for accurate timekeeping, qibla determination, and pilgrimage route mapping spurred advancements in astronomy and geography."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-10T17:23:09Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#42861733994", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Advanced mathematical knowledge used by subsequent scholars", "type": "Idea", "year": ""}, {"title": "Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati", "type": "Movement", "year": "Medieval"}], "newValue": ["Al-Marwarrūdhī's work contributed to a more accurate understanding of the Earth's size, influencing subsequent geographical and astronomical studies.", "His 